## 1. The three layers

```
 Your game
    |   k2.draw_texture(...), k2.load_texture_from_file(...), k2.set_shader(...)
    v
 karl2d.odin  (backend-independent)
    - owns State: CPU vertex buffer, list of Draw_Calls, "current" settings
    - owns CPU-side asset data: Shader reflection info, font glyph tables, font atlas pixels
    - hands out small value types (Texture, Shader, Font, Render_Texture) that wrap handles
    |
    |   rb.load_texture / rb.load_shader / rb.draw / rb.clear / rb.present ...
    v
 Render_Backend_Interface  (render_backend_interface.odin, a struct of proc pointers)
    |
    v
 render_backend_d3d11.odin
    - owns D3D11_State: device, context, swapchain, one dynamic vertex buffer
    - owns GPU objects in three handle maps: textures, render_targets, shaders
```

Two design decisions shape everything else:

- **The core never touches a GPU object.** It holds opaque handles (`Texture_Handle`, `Shader_Handle`, `Render_Target_Handle`, 
    all `distinct hm.Handle64`). The backend resolves a handle to a real object with `hm.get` at the moment it needs it.
- **Drawing is deferred.** 
	- Draw procs only append bytes to a CPU buffer and bookkeeping to a `Draw_Call`. 
	- The backend sees nothing until a flush, when it receives the whole buffer and the whole draw call list in a single `rb.draw` call.

The backend is picked at compile time in `render_backend_chooser.odin`. On Windows the default is `"d3d11"`, overridable with `-define:KARL2D_RENDER_BACKEND=gl`.

