## 4. Assets: loading, storing, retrieving

### 4.1 The ownership model

Karl2D has no asset manager. There is no cache keyed by filename, no reference counting, and no registry 
of loaded textures or shaders in the core. Loading the same file twice gives two independent GPU textures.

| Asset | What you hold | CPU-side storage | GPU-side storage (D3D11) | Retrieved by |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Texture | `Texture {handle, width, height}` by value | none; pixels are not kept after upload | `D3D11_Texture` in `s.textures` | handle, via `hm.get` in the backend |
| Image | `Image {pixels, width, height}` by value | `pixels` in `s.allocator`; you own it | none | direct slice access |
| Render texture | `Render_Texture {texture, render_target}` by value | none | `D3D11_Texture` + `D3D11_Render_Target` | two handles |
| Shader | `Shader` struct by value | reflection data, constants buffer, lookup maps, all in `s.allocator`, referenced by slices/maps inside the struct | `D3D11_Shader` in `s.shaders` | handle for GPU side; struct fields for CPU side |
| Static font | `Font` (an `int` index) | `Font_Data` in `s.fonts`: glyph table and ranges | its own atlas texture | index into `s.fonts` |
| Dynamic font | `Font` (an `int` index) | `Font_Data` in `s.fonts`: a copy of the TTF bytes, kerning cache; glyph pixels in the shared `s.font_cache` | one shared atlas texture, `s.font_atlas_texture` | index into `s.fonts`, then glyph key into `font_cache.glyphs` |

Because `Texture`, `Shader` and `Render_Texture` are plain values, copying them is cheap and all copies refer to the same underlying object. A `Shader` copy shares its slices and maps with the original, which is why `set_shader_constant(shd, ...)` on your copy is visible through `s.current_shader`.

Fonts are the exception to "the core stores nothing": they live in `s.fonts`, a dynamic array that only grows. `destroy_font` frees a slot's contents but does not remove or reuse the slot.

### 4.2 Textures

All four public loaders converge on `rb.load_texture`:

```odin
load_texture_from_file(filename, options)          // karl2d.odin:1941
read_entire_file(filename, frame_allocator)        // file bytes -> frame arena
image.load_from_bytes(..., frame_allocator)        // decoded pixels -> frame arena
load_texture_from_bytes_raw(pixels, w, h, .RGBA_8_Norm)
rb.load_texture(s.rb_state, bytes, w, h, format)  -> Texture_Handle
    return Texture { handle, width, height }
load_texture_from_bytes(bytes, options)              // karl2d.odin:1978   (same, minus the file read)
load_texture_from_bytes_raw(bytes, w, h, format)     // karl2d.odin:2007   (no decoding; you state the format)
load_texture_from_image(image)                       // karl2d.odin:2045   (validates size, uploads as RGBA8)
create_texture(w, h, format)                         // karl2d.odin:1908   (empty texture via rb.create_texture)
```

Details:

- Decoding uses Odin's `core:image` with the PNG, JPEG, BMP and TGA loaders registered (see the `_ :: jpeg` etc. imports at `karl2d.odin:7729`).
- `.alpha_add_if_missing` is always set. `.Premultiply_Alpha` in `Load_Texture_Options` adds `.alpha_premultiply`, for use with `set_blend_mode(.Premultiplied_Alpha)`.
- Both the file bytes and the decoded pixels go into the **frame allocator**. They are not freed explicitly; they disappear at the next `reset_frame_allocator` (called by `update`). The GPU copy is the only lasting one.
- On failure the loaders log and return a zero `Texture`. `draw_texture_fit` returns early for `TEXTURE_NONE` or zero size, so a failed texture draws nothing.

Mutating and destroying:

- `update_texture(tex, bytes, rect, pitch)` (`:2159`) forwards to `rb.update_texture`.
- `set_texture_filter` / `set_texture_filter_ex` (`:2183`) forward to `rb.set_texture_filter`.
- `destroy_texture` (`:2166`) forwards to `rb.destroy_texture`.

All three first call `_flush_if_batch_uses_texture(handle)` (`:7467`). Since drawing is deferred, pending draw calls may still refer to the texture, so they must reach the GPU before the texture changes or disappears. The helper scans `s.current_draw_call.textures` and every recorded draw call's `textures`, and flushes only if it finds the handle.

### 4.3 Images

`Image` is the CPU-only counterpart: `[]Color` plus dimensions. `load_image_from_bytes` (`:2103`) decodes into the frame allocator, checks for 8-bit, 4-channel data, then copies the pixels into `s.allocator` so they outlive the frame. You free them with `destroy_image`. Images are used for window icons, custom cursors and as input to `load_texture_from_image`. They never reach the renderer directly.

### 4.4 Render textures

`create_render_texture(w, h)` (`:4355`) calls `rb.create_render_texture`, which returns two handles. They are packaged as:

```odin
Render_Texture :: struct {
    texture:       Texture,               // sample it like any other texture
    render_target: Render_Target_Handle,  // draw into it
}
```

`set_render_texture` (`:4396`) does not touch the GPU. It stores the target handle and size in `s.current_render_target*`, sets the `.Render_Target` and `.Scissor` change bits, and rebuilds the projection matrix for the new size (which also sets `.Constants`).

`destroy_render_texture` (`:4378`) flushes if any pending draw call renders into the target or samples its texture, then destroys both backend objects.

### 4.5 Shaders

`load_shader_from_file` (`:5143`) reads the vertex and fragment files into the frame allocator (reading once if both names are equal) and calls `load_shader_from_bytes` (`:5178`), which is where the two halves of a shader are assembled.

**Step 1: the backend compiles and reflects.**

```odin
handle, desc, ok := rb.load_shader(s.rb_state, vs_bytes, fs_bytes, s.frame_allocator, layout_formats)
```

The backend returns a handle plus a `Shader_Desc`, allocated in the frame allocator:

```odin
Shader_Desc :: struct {
    constants:          []Shader_Constant_Desc,          // name + size in bytes
    texture_bindpoints: []Shader_Texture_Bindpoint_Desc, // name
    inputs:             []Shader_Input,                  // name, register, type, format
}
```

**Step 2: the core builds its own bookkeeping from the description**, in `s.allocator`:

| `Shader` field | Built from | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `constants_data: []u8` | sum of all constant sizes | one flat CPU buffer holding every constant's current value |
| `constants: []Shader_Constant_Location` | running offset over `desc.constants` | offset + size of constant *i* in `constants_data`; index *i* matches the backend's constant *i* |
| `constant_lookup: map[string]...` | constant names | `shader.constant_lookup["name"]` gives a location for `set_shader_constant` |
| `constant_builtin_locations` | a constant named `view_projection` | where the core writes its view-projection matrix |
| `texture_bindpoints: []Texture_Handle` | one slot per reflected texture | textures set with `set_shader_texture` |
| `texture_lookup: map[string]int` | texture names | name to bindpoint index |
| `default_texture_index` | a texture named `tex` | the slot that receives the texture passed to `draw_texture*` |
| `inputs` | cloned from `desc.inputs` | vertex layout |
| `default_input_offsets` | inputs named `position`, `texcoord`, `color` with matching types | byte offsets `batch_vertex` writes to; `-1` if absent |
| `input_overrides` | one per input | values set with `override_shader_input` |
| `vertex_size` | sum of `pixel_format_size(input.format)` | bytes per vertex |

The contract between a shader and the core is therefore entirely by **name**: `view_projection`, `tex`, `position`, `texcoord`, `color`. A custom shader that uses those names gets the built-in behaviour for free.

The input formats come from `get_shader_input_format` (`:7555`) unless you pass `layout_formats`:

| Input | CPU format | Bytes |
|---|---|---|
| `position` (Vec2 or Vec3) | `.RG_32_Float`, or `.RGB_32_Float` when `depth_test` is on | 8 or 12 |
| `texcoord` (Vec2) | `.RG_32_Float` | 8 |
| `color` (Vec4) | `.RGBA_8_Norm` | 4 |
| anything else | float format matching the component count | 4 per component |

So the default vertex is **20 bytes** (24 with depth testing), which gives about 50,000 vertices or 8,300 quads per batch.

Setting shader state:

- `set_shader` (`:5312`) copies the struct into `s.current_shader` and sets `.Shader`, `.Constants` and `.Textures`.
- `set_shader_constant` (`:5334`) validates size and copies the value into `shd.constants_data`, then sets `.Constants`.
- `set_shader_texture` (`:5368`) stores a handle in `shd.texture_bindpoints` and sets `.Textures`.
- `override_shader_input` (`:5396`) stores bytes in `shader.input_overrides`. It sets no change bit, because the value is written into each vertex.

None of these call the backend. The values only reach the GPU as part of a `Draw_Call`.

`destroy_shader` (`:5268`) flushes if a pending draw call uses the shader, calls `rb.destroy_shader`, then frees all the CPU-side slices, maps and cloned names.

### 4.6 Fonts

`Font` is `distinct int`, an index into `s.fonts: [dynamic]Font_Data`. There are two kinds.

**Static fonts** (`load_static_font_from_bytes`, `:4698`) are baked once at load time:

1. `stbtt.InitFont` parses the TTF. Codepoints default to ASCII 32 to 126.
2. Each glyph is rasterised with `stbtt.GetGlyphBitmap` at the requested pixel size.
3. `stb_rect_pack` packs the glyphs, starting with a 128×128 atlas and doubling up to 4096×4096 until they fit.
4. The coverage bitmaps are copied into an RGBA atlas as `{255, 255, 255, a}`, or `{a, a, a, a}` with `premultiply_alpha`.
5. The atlas is uploaded with `load_texture_from_image`, so a static font is an ordinary texture as far as the backend is concerned.
6. The glyph table (sorted by codepoint) and the list of contiguous codepoint ranges are cloned into `s.allocator`. All the working memory was in the frame allocator.

At draw time, `draw_text_static` finds the glyph by scanning the ranges, then calls `draw_texture_fit` with the font's atlas and the glyph's source rect. A missing glyph draws a small red rectangle.

**Dynamic fonts** (`load_dynamic_font_from_bytes`, `:4991`) rasterise on demand. Loading only clones the TTF bytes and parses the header (`fc.init_font`). The pixels live in the shared `s.font_cache` (`font_cache/font_cache.odin`):

- One CPU atlas, `cache.pixels: [][4]u8`, starting at 256×256, shared by all dynamic fonts.
- `cache.glyphs: map[Glyph_Key]Glyph`, where the key packs `font id << 48 | size << 32 | codepoint`. Every size of every glyph is a separate entry.
- A skyline packer (ported from Fontstash) that places new glyphs.
- A dirty rectangle (`dirty_min`, `dirty_max`) covering everything written since the last upload.

The GPU copy of the atlas is `s.font_atlas_texture`. The flow in `draw_text_dynamic` (`:1789`):

1. `_font_render_size` multiplies the font size by the camera zoom and rounds, so glyphs are rasterised at the size they will appear on screen.
2. `_sync_font_atlas_texture` (`:7663`) recreates the GPU texture if its size no longer matches the CPU atlas.
3. `fc.place_text_iterate` walks the text. For each codepoint, `fc.get_glyph` returns the cached glyph or rasterises it into the CPU atlas with `stbtt.MakeGlyphBitmap` and grows the dirty rectangle.
4. Each placed glyph becomes a `draw_texture_fit` call that samples `s.font_atlas_texture`.

At this point the quads are recorded but the new glyph pixels exist only on the CPU. The upload is deferred to the flush: `draw_current_batch` calls `_update_font_atlas` (`:7684`) immediately before `rb.draw`, which sends just the dirty rectangle through `rb.update_texture` using the `pitch` parameter to skip the rest of each row.

When the packer has no room, the iterator returns `.No_Room` and the caller:

1. Flushes the batch, because pending quads refer to current atlas positions.
2. Calls `fc.make_room`, which doubles the atlas (up to 4096×4096) or, at maximum size, compacts it by keeping roughly the most recently used half of the glyphs and repacking them. Compaction is allowed at most once per timestamp.
3. Re-syncs the GPU texture and retries the same glyph.

`measure_text` for dynamic fonts runs the same iterator, so measuring text can also rasterise glyphs and trigger a flush.

`destroy_font` (`:5043`) destroys the atlas texture for a static font. For a dynamic font it removes the font's glyphs from the cache map and frees the TTF copy.

